    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm currently working on a digital preservation project for a series of 19th-century topographic maps. We're using Dublin Core for the metadata schema, but I'm running into a bit of a dilemma regarding the \"Creator\" field. We have several plates where the engraver or cartographer is completely unidentified by the archival record. \n\nWhat are the current best practices or standard conventions for handling these unidentified creators within a Dublin Core framework? I want to ensure we maintain archival integrity without leaving the fields blank or using non-standard placeholders that might break interoperability during future harvesting.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When dealing with unidentified creators, it's recommended to use the \"Rights Holder\" field instead, as it's also applicable to works without explicit creators. However, since this field is not always recreate-able, Dublin Core also provides the \"Other\" group for miscellaneous metadata that can be applied to the \"Creator\" field. You can use the Dublin Core Element Set extension, specifically the \"creator ident-unknown\" , to explicitly mark the creator field as unknown. This will allow you to maintain archival integrity while following standard conventions.",
      "source": "target",
